  "summary": "Malicious code in super-color (PyPI)",
  "details": "\n---\n_-= Per source details. Do not edit below this line.=-_\n\n## Source: amazon-inspector (9f011b9543cf75605eb55a45f8178b552c3a9371687bd9ff6112275b67290cf8)\nsuper-color presents itself as a terminal color library but, on Windows, every advertised Color method (paint/print/__call__, e.g. colors.red.print(...), colors.green(x)) invokes an internal _run_payload() routine that fetches a Windows executable from https://cdn.discordapp.com/attachments/1442967767853563904/1568948904123310080/loader.exe with three fallbacks (urllib with a Chrome User-Agent, curl, and certutil -urlcache -split -f), writes the bytes to %TEMP%\\SolaceRuntime\\python315.exe after verifying the MZ header, and launches the file detached via subprocess.Popen with DETACHED_PROCESS | CREATE_NEW_PROCESS_GROUP | CREATE_NO_WINDOW. The staging directory and filename impersonate a Python runtime, the LOLBin fallback chain and hidden-window flags are standard Windows commodity-dropper evasion, and the fetched payload is attacker-controlled content hosted on a Discord CDN unrelated to the package's stated purpose. Normal usage of the library on Windows therefore results in an attacker-controlled PE executing on the installer's machine.\n\n## Source: kam193 (908af2bf828b75158c5bf0aae5a9d422694f21f566a745ff42b82f1f7ed4560d)\nPackage hides a code downloading and installing an infostealer.\n\n\n---\n\nCategory: MALICIOUS - The campaign has clearly malicious intent, like infostealers.\n\n\nCampaign: 2026-11-chromacli\n\n\nReasons (based on the campaign):\n\n\n - infostealer\n\n\n - Downloads and executes a remote executable.\n\n\n - action-hidden-in-lib-usage\n\n\n - malware\n",
